</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y^{\prime}}:(L,D^{\prime})\leftarrow\bm{y}\odot\bm{x^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y^{\prime}}:(L,D^{\prime})\leftarrow\bm{y}\odot\bm{x^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X_{sorted}^{\prime}}:(L,D)\leftarrow Linear^{2}(\bm{y^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒅</mi></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X_{sorted}^{\prime}}:(L,D)\leftarrow Linear^{2}(\bm{y^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
             <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X^{\prime}}:(L,D)\leftarrow\bm{X_{sorted}^{\prime}}[\bm{I_{reverse}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒅</mi></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mrow><mi>𝒓</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒓</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒆</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X^{\prime}}:(L,D)\leftarrow\bm{X_{sorted}^{\prime}}[\bm{I_{reverse}}]</annotation></semantics></math> {Reverse sort output}

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph-Mamba incorporates Mamba’s selection mechanism from Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Graph-dependent selection mechanism ‣ 3 Graph-Mamba ‣ Graph-Mamba: Towards Long-Range Graph Sequence Modeling with Selective State Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> into the GraphGPS framework. Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Graph-Mamba ‣ Graph-Mamba: Towards Long-Range Graph Sequence Modeling with Selective State Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> A illustrates Graph-Mamba’s adaptation of the GPS layer, where the attention module is replaced by GMB, denoted as a GMB layer. We used the GatedGCN model as the default for MPNN for local context selection, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Graph-Mamba ‣ Graph-Mamba: Towards Long-Range Graph Sequence Modeling with Selective State Spaces" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> B. The GatedGCN model aggregates information from neighboring nodes defined by edge connections, and employs a gating mechanism to decide on how much of that information to incorporate, inspired by RNNs. GatedGCN and GMB collectively contribute to the overarching theme of recurrence-based context selection in Graph-Mamba, facilitating node filtering within the local neighborhood and among the global connections. The graph feature computation with SE and PE prior to the GMB layers remain consistent. The GMB layers thus receive the SE/PE-aware node and edge embeddings as input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A Graph-Mamba framework consists of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> stacked GMB layers. Algorithm 1 defines the GMB function (more explanations in Sections 3.4-3.6), and Algorithm 2 illustrates the forward pass through <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> GMB layers. In Algorithm 2, each GMB layer performs two round of embedding updates using MPNN and GMB, given an input graph of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> nodes, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> edges, and embedding size <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, an MPNN updates both node and edge embeddings (line 2), while GMB updates the node embeddings only (line 3). The updated node embeddings from an MPNN (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}^{k+1}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}^{k+1}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>) and GMB (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{X}^{k+1}_{GMB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{X}^{k+1}_{GMB}</annotation></semantics></math>) are combined through an MLP layer to produce the output node embeddings (line 6). Using the output from the previous layer as the input for the next layer, this process iterates through <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> GMB layers to obtain the final output node embeddings, which are subsequently used for downstream tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Node prioritization strategy for non-sequential graph input</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark of Graph-Mamba on Long-Range Graph Datasets</span> with existing methods. These five datasets feature large input graphs with 150 to 1,400 nodes. Best results are colored in <span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">first</span>, <span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF7D00;">second</span>, <span id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#6E73FF;">third</span>.</figcaption>
